title: Any of three
mode: draw
chapter: Combining gates
check: behaves
parts: and or not
---- intro
You chained two AND gates to ask "are all three on?". Chain two **OR** gates and you ask the other question: "is **any** of them on?".

The light should be off in one row only, when all three switches are off.
---- task
Light `y` when at least one of `a`, `b` and `c` is on.
---- start
input a 0 0
input b 0 60
input c 0 120
led y 360 60
---- reference
input a, b, c
led y
y = a | b | c
---- answer
input a 0 0
input b 0 60
input c 0 120
or o1 120 20
or o2 240 60
led y 360 60
a -> o1.0
b -> o1.1
o1 -> o2.0
c -> o2.1
o2 -> y
---- hint1
It is the "all three" circuit with OR gates instead of AND.
---- hint2
The first OR gets `a` and `b`; the second gets its output and `c`.
---- solution
OR `a` with `b`, then OR that with `c`. Any one switch is enough to light `y`.
---- done
AND chains ask "all of them?", OR chains ask "any of them?". A lot of logic is one of those two questions.
